# "Historic Structure Report: Physical Fabric Section, Leister Barn, Gettysburg National Military Park" (a National Park Service report of 1985-86; the cover and the date are not in the text layer): second reading, workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15.
# The first reading quoted four passages from the reading-order text, which interleaves lines of this typescript. The layout text (<ID>.layout.txt) reads in order (with letter-spacing, which the cleaned quote ignores), so every quote here is cut from the layout text (flag ~L) in cleaned form.
# PDF pp. 13, 14, 15, 18, 21, 23, 25, 29, 47, 62 and 70 were looked at as page images and every digit was read from the image (the plus sign with a bar of the roofing sentences reads as the plus-or-minus sign).
# The report studies the barn through the photographs of 1863 (Alexander Gardner, shortly after the battle), 1866-68 (Tyson), 1882 and about 1900 (Tipton) and measures the standing fabric of 1985. A statement is dated by the photograph or the part it describes: what the report says of the Gardner photographs is the barn as it stood a few days after the battle; the Tyson, Tipton and measured-fabric statements are carried with their own dates. The measured dimensions are of the barn as it stands: the log bay and the threshing floor bay are the two bays of the barn of 1863; the south bay replaced the shed in about 1880-82.
# CORRECTION of the first reading: the first definition of PDF p. 25 (xid NPSX-HSR-LEISTER-BARN-p025-01, in 90-farm-studies.xdef) had deleted the word 'not' of the sentence 'appears not to have changed' (the reading-order text had displaced it); it is now cut from the layout text and the page image of p. 25 was looked at (see CORRECTIONS.md).
@ NPS-HSR-LEISTER-BARN
%basis the Gardner, Tyson and Tipton photographs and the report's own survey of the standing fabric, as the report names them
%images 13, 14, 15, 18, 21, 23, 25, 29, 47, 62, 70

13 | buildings | Bishop's farm before 1861: 10 acres, a 1 1/2-story log house, a small log barn and small outbuildings | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-HOUSE1,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1840-1861 (Bishop's farm, before Mrs. Leister bought it) | stated | The report says Bishop's farm, small even by the standards of the 1800s, held 10 acres, a 1 1/2-story log house, a small log barn and several small outbuildings | =Bishop's farm, small even by 1800s standards, encompassed 10 acres of land, a 1 1/2-story log house, small log {{bam,|barn,}} and several small outbuildings. ~L
13 | general | Mrs. Leister bought the farm from Bishop in 1861 | bld-leister-house | 1861 | stated | The report says Bishop lived on the farm until 1861 when he sold it to Mrs. Leister for $900.00 | =Bishop lived on the farm until 1861 when he sold it to Mrs. Leister for $900.00. ~L
13 | hospitals | Leister buildings doubtless also a temporary field hospital | bld-leister-house,H-16,STR-LEISTER-BARN1,STR-LEISTER-HOUSE1 | 1863-07 (hedged: doubtless) | hedged | The report says that doubtless the Leister buildings were also used as a temporary field hospital during the battle | =Doubtless, the buildings were also used as a {{tenporary|temporary}} field hospital during the battle. ~L
13 | damage | Mrs. Leister found the farm in literal ruins: dead horses, trampled crops, barn and house damaged by shell fire, siding taken for firewood and grave markers | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1,STR-LEISTER-HOUSE1 | 1863-07 (several days after the battle) | stated | The report says Mrs. Leister left the farm on the evening of July 1, returned several days later and found it in literal ruins, with dead horses in the yard, the crops trampled and both barn and house damaged by shell fire and by troops who took the siding for firewood and grave markers | =Mrs. Leister had left the farm on the evening of July 1 and when she returned several days later, she found the farm in literal ruins. Dead horses littered the yard, the crops were trampled, and both the {{b a m|barn}} and house damaged by shell fire and troops who scrounged the siding for firewood and grave markers. ~L
14 | buildings | The barn was repaired with new siding within weeks of the battle | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | !1863-07 to 1863-08 (repair within a few weeks after the battle; not the barn of the battle) | stated | The report says the house was repaired within a few weeks of the battle and the barn was also repaired with new siding | =The house was repaired within a few weeks and the barn was also repaired with new siding. ~L
14 | buildings | In 1874 the house got a two-story addition and the barn's roof was raised and the lean-to wagon shed incorporated into the barn | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-HOUSE1,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | !1874 (additions after the battle; not in the 1863 scene) | stated | The report says that in 1874 a two-story addition was put on the east gable of the house and that the barn was also added onto in that period, the roof being raised and the lean-to wagon shed incorporated into the barn | =In 1874, a two-story addition was put on the east gable of the house; the {{b a m|barn}} was also added onto during this period, in that the roof was raised and the lean-to wagon shed incorporated into the {{b a m .|barn.}} ~L
15 | buildings | The Civil War barn was two bays with the appended shed and a lower roof of a different configuration | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (barn of the battle; conclusion of the 1985 study) | stated | The report concludes that the Civil War barn was in fact two bays with the appended shed addition and a roof of lower and different configuration | =Subsequently, it has been ascertained that the Civil War {{b a m|barn}} was, in fact, two bays with the appended shed addition and a roof of lower and different configuration. ~L
15 | buildings | The earlier study of 1961 had assumed three bays, the south lean-to shed being an addition (superseded) | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | ~1961 (the earlier reading; the report finds it wrong for 1863) | conflict | The report says Tilberg's study of 1961 called for preservation of the barn to its war-time appearance on the assumption that the south lean-to shed had been an addition to an existing three bay barn, an assumption the report's own finding (the Civil War barn was two bays with an appended shed) replaces | =As a case-in-point, Frederick Tilberg's 1961 Study (Historic Structures Report, Leister House & Farm Buildings) called for the preservation of the {{b a m|barn}} to its war time appearance and was based on the assumption that the south lean-to shed had been an addition to the existing three bay {{bam.|barn.}} ~L
17 | buildings | Gardner's photographs of 1863: siding mostly removed during the fighting, structural framing intact | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photographs shortly after the battle) | stated | The report says the Gardner photographs were taken shortly after the battle, as the unburied horses show, and that the close-up of the barn and chicken house shows that most of the siding had been removed during the fighting while the structural framing was intact | =The Gardner photographs were taken shortly after the battle, as attested to by the still unburied carcasses of horses in his farmstead view. The close-up of the barn and chicken house reveals the full extent of the damage to the large structure — although most of the siding had been removed during the fighting, the structural framing was intact. ~L
17 | buildings | Tyson's view of 1866-68: reroofed and reclad but the same configuration as Gardner's, two bays to the north and a south lean-to shed | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | ~1866-1868 (Tyson view; the report says the configuration is the same as in the 1863 Gardner views) | stated | The report says Tyson's view of several years later shows a reroofed and reclad building whose configuration is the same as Gardner's: two bays to the north with a south lean-to shed | =Tyson's view of several years later reveals a reroofed and reclad building, but the configuration is the same as that recorded by Gardner: two bays to the north with a south lean-to shed. ~L
18 | buildings | Barn roof of 1863: drawn shakes about 12 inches to the weather, lapped vertically and horizontally across the course | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph) | stated | The report says the roofing on the higher barn bays is shown in the Gardner photographs as drawn shakes of about 12 inches of exposure, lapped both vertically and horizontally across the course | ={{Hie|The}} roofing on the higher {{b a m|barn}} bays is shown as drawn shakes, {{+12"|±12"}} exposure, lapped both vertically and horizontally across the course. ~L
18 | buildings | Shed roof of 1863: drawn shakes laid the normal way, side edges butted, about 12 inches of vertical exposure | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph; the report says it appears to have been so covered) | hedged | The report says the shed roof appears in the Gardner photographs to have been covered with drawn shakes laid in the normal manner, with side edges butted and about 12 inches of vertical exposure | =The shed roof, however, appears to have been covered with drawn shakes laid in the "normal" manner — i.e., side edges butted and with {{+12"|±12"}} vertical exposure. ~L
18 | buildings | Barn ridge combed toward the east and the roof pitch flatter than today's, in the Gardner photographs | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph) | stated | The report says that in the Gardner photographs the ridge was combed toward the east, the roofing at the hip rafter between shed roof and main roof toward the southwest, and that Photograph 3 shows the roof pitch flatter than the roof of today | ={{Hie|The}} ridge was combed toward the east, although the roofing adjacent to the hip rafter between the shed roof and main roof appears to have been {{ocmbed|combed}} toward the southwest. Photograph 3 also reveals that the roof pitch was flatter than that which exists today. ~L
18 | buildings | Siding boards left in the Gardner photographs were white-washed or painted, had no battens and were not uniform in width | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph) | stated | The report says that less than two dozen siding boards were left on the two elevations Gardner recorded and that they were white-washed or painted, had no battens, and were not uniform in width, several being wedge-form | =Less than two dozen siding boards (excluding the gable) were left on the two elevations recorded by Gardner. Study of these reveals the following: 1. They were {{vhite-washed|white-washed}} or painted; 2. They had no battens; and, 3. They were not uniform in width and several did not have parallel edges, i.e., were wedge-form. ~L
19 | buildings | Shed addition of 1863: a single exterior opening on the south and east elevations, a door at the southeast corner | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph) | stated | The report says the exposed framing of the shed addition in the Gardner photograph reveals that there was but a single exterior opening on the south and east elevations, a door at the southeast corner | =The visible exposed structural framing of the shed addition reveals that there was but a single exterior opening on the south and east elevations, a door at the southeast corner. ~L
21 | buildings | Log walls of the east elevation, north bay: chinking only in part at the time of the Gardner view | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph) | stated | The report says the log walls of the east elevation, north bay, retained only part of their chinking at the time of the Gardner view | =The log walls of the east elevation, north bay, retained only part of their chinking at the time of the Gardner view. What is evident is white-washed. ~L
21 | fences | Eastern barnyard of post and rail fences and a wall and rider fence, with a stone wall south of the barn interrupted by a post and rail section | FEN-leister,bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph; the report calls it the post-war view) | stated | The report says the view it calls post-war, discussed under the Gardner photographs, shows the barn with an eastern barnyard formed by post and rail fences and wall and rider fences, the stone wall continuing south of the barn and interrupted by a post and rail section possibly serving as a gate, crossed by another post and rail fence running west, with no gate beside the forebay | =The post-war view indicates that the barn had an eastern barnyard formed by both post and rail and wall and rider fences. The stone wall continued south of the {{bam|barn,}} interrupted by a section of post and rail fence, possibly serving as a gate, which was intersected by another post and rail fence extending westward. There was no gate adjacent to the forebay; the north forebay door served in that function. ~L
21 | orchards | Fruit trees with white-washed trunks east of the barnyard, surely an orchard in two groupings | bld-leister-house | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph) | hedged | The report says many fruit trees with white-washed trunks are visible east of the barnyard in the Gardner photograph, surely an orchard laid out in two groupings | =East of the barnyard many fruit trees with white-washed trunks are visible. Surely an orchard, they were laid out in two groupings ~L
23 | orchards | The orchard trees nearest the barn appear not to have been apples but possibly pears or cherries | bld-leister-house | 1863-07 (Gardner photograph) | hedged | The report says the trees of the grouping nearest the barn appear not to have been apples, but possibly pears or cherries | ={{bam|barn}} appear not to have been apples, but possibly pears or cherries. ~L | @21: those nearest the
23 | buildings | Tyson stereoview of 1866-68: the barn's original overall appearance had not been altered | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | ~1866-1868 (Tyson view) | stated | The report says the Tyson stereoview of 1866-68 shows the west and south elevations in sharp focus with a new covering of siding but reveals that the barn's original overall appearance had not been altered | =This photograph is useful because it shows the west and south elevations in sharp focus with a new covering of siding, but reveals that the barn's original overall appearance had not been altered. ~L | TYSON STEREOVIEW: 1866-68
23 | buildings | Tyson view: the roof not yet raised, still of flatter pitch, with the original biaxially tapered shakes on the ridge roof | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | ~1866-1868 (Tyson view; the report treats it as the barn of 1863) | stated | The report says that in the Tyson view the roof had not been raised and still has a flatter pitch than the existing roof, the original biaxially tapered shakes remaining on the ridge roof though the shed roof appears to have been recovered | =The roof had not been raised in this view, and still retains a flatter pitch than that of the existing roof. The original biaxially tapered shakes on the ridge roof remain, although the shed roof appears to have been recovered. ~L | TYSON STEREOVIEW: 1866-68
24 | buildings | Tyson view, like Gardner's: no openings on the south elevation, only the threshing floor doors on the west | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | ~1866-1868 (Tyson view; the same as Gardner's of 1863) | stated | The report says the Tyson view, like Gardner's, shows no openings on the south elevation and only the threshing floor doors on the west | =The Tyson view, like Gardner's, shows no openings {{chi|on}} the south elevation and only the threshing floor doors on the west. ~L
29 | orchards | The ragged orchard first viewed in the Gardner document appears re-established to its original extent in the later view | bld-leister-house | ~about 1900 (later view; the orchard first seen in Gardner's 1863 photograph) | hedged | The report says that in the later view the fencing and wall pattern remains the same as in the earlier photographs and the ragged orchard first viewed in the Gardner document appears re-established to its original extent | ={{Hie|The}} fencing and wall pattern remains the same as that shown in the earlier photographs, and the ragged orchard first viewed in the Gardner document {{i appears|appears}} reestablished to its original extent. ~L
29 | buildings | Leister barn about 60 yards west of the house, 37 feet 8 inches by 22 feet 11 inches, three asymmetrical bays (as measured in 1985) | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | ~1985 (the barn as it stands: the two northern bays are of 1863, the south bay of about 1880) | stated | The report says the barn is sited some 60 yards west of the Leister house with its long axis north/south, with overall dimensions of 37'8" by 22'11" and three asymmetrical bays: a northern log portion with a frame overshoot and two post and frame bents to the south | ={{Hie Leister B a m|The Leister Barn}} is sited some 60 yards west of the Leister House and oriented with its long axis north/south and perpendicular to the house. Its overall dimensions are {{37'8"x22,H|37'8"x22'11",}} divided into three asymmetrical bays by a northern log portion with superimposed frame overshoot and by two post and frame bents to the south. ~L
29 | buildings | End bays with frame storage lofts; the northern log bay with stalls and mangers | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | ~1985 (the barn as it stands; the northern log bay is of 1863) | stated | The report says the end bays have frame storage lofts and the northern log bay has stalls and mangers | ={{Hie|The}} end bays have frame storage lofts, and the northern log bay has stalls and mangers. ~L
47 | buildings | Log bay 14 feet 2 inches by 17 feet 8 inches, about 12 feet of barked rough-hewn oak logs with V-notched corners on a rough fieldstone foundation | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863 (original log bay as measured in 1985) | stated | The report says the log portion of the barn is 14'2" deep and 17'8" wide, its logs rising about 12 feet above a rough field stone foundation, the oak logs being barked, rough hewn and joined at V-notched corners | =The log portion of the {{bam|barn}} is 14'2" deep and 17'8" wide, the logs extending above the rough field stone foundation to a height of approximately 12 feet. The oak logs are barked, rough hewn, and joined at V-notched corners. ~L
47 | buildings | Chinking of diagonally laid flat stones and small logs in a lime, clay, sand and hair mortar, typical of Adams County | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863 (original chinking; twentieth century patches in Portland cement are later) | stated | The report says the chinking between the logs is typical of Adams County, diagonally laid flat stones and small diameter logs in a matrix of lime, clay, sand and hair mortar | =The chinking between the logs is typical of that found in Adams County, a {{canbination|combination}} of diagonally laid flat stones and small diameter logs in a matrix of lime, clay, sand, and hair mortar. ~L
62 | buildings | Threshing floor bay 13 feet 3 inches deep and 22 feet 11 inches wide, doors on the west and a framed wall on the east | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863 (the threshing floor bay is original; dimensions measured in 1985) | stated | The report says the threshing floor bay is 13'3" deep and 22'11" wide, its primary frame 17'9" wide with an eastern extension adding 5'2" | =The central or threshing floor bay is 13'3" deep and 22'11" wide, the primary frame is 17'9" wide, and the eastern extension adds an additional 5'2". ~L
62 | buildings | The threshing floor bay: log stall bay to the north, a post and frame bent to the south, a large pair of doors on the west and a 5 1/2 by 4 foot door in the east wall | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863 (the threshing floor bay is original; description of 1985) | stated | The report says the threshing floor bay is bounded on the north by the log stall bay and on the south by a post and frame bent, with a large pair of doors on the west spanning from grade to 4'6" below the eave and a sided frame wall with a 5 1/2 by 4 foot door on the east | =It is defined on the north by the log stall bay, on the south by a post and frame bent, on the west by a large pair of doors spanning from grade to just 4'6" below the eave, and on the east by a sided frame wall incorporating a 5 1/2'X4' door. ~L
62 | buildings | A hewn oak joist reused in the loft floor came from the original south shed bay, removed by 1882 | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | 1863 (original shed bay frame) to 1882 (removal) | stated | The report says the reused hewn oak joist has been identified as part of the frame of the original south shed bay of the barn, which was removed by 1882 | =that member has been identified as part of the frame of the original south shed bay of the barn which was removed by 1882. ~L
70 | buildings | The south bay (wagon shed) dates to about 1880 and incorporates some framing of the original one-story shed | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | !about 1880 (south bay; the shed of 1863 was a one-story lean-to) | stated | The report says that, as the photographs make clear, the existing south bay dates to about 1880, though some of the original one-story shed framing was incorporated into the later east elevation and vestigial material remains in the south elevation of the threshing floor bent | =As the photographic record makes clear, the existing {{90uth|south}} bay dates to c.1880, although examination reveals that some of the original one story shed framing was incorporated into the later east elevation and that a considerable amount of vestigial material remains in the south elevation of the threshing floor bent. ~L
70 | buildings | The south bay as it stands is 10 feet deep and 22 feet 11 inches wide | bld-leister-house,STR-LEISTER-BARN1 | !about 1880 (south bay as it stands; the shed it replaced was of equal area according to the report) | stated | The report says the existing south bay is 10'0" deep and 22'11" wide | =The existing south bay is 10'0" deep and 22'11" wide. ~L
